items.body:
Priority: **P0 / High**
Target: **v0.1**
Depends on/coordinates with: multi-credential LLM pool issue created alongside this issue
Related: #3851, #3674, #3687, #3844, #3673

## Product requirement

An agent must be able to change model according to task, availability or user choice **without ever owning provider authentication or routing credentials**.

Model choice and provider authentication are separate concerns:

```text
Agent/Hermes
  chooses logical model
        |
        v
TXO AI Gateway
  authenticates caller
  checks tenant/agent model policy
  resolves tenant-owned provider/credential pool
  load-balances/fails over
        |
        v
upstream provider/model
```

## Current limitation

AgentIdentity model virtual keys are currently minted for exactly one model:
`models: ["txo-default"]`.

That is too restrictive for real agents which may need to switch model, while simply allowing every gateway model would be too permissive.

## Desired contract

Define a tenant-owned logical model catalog and agent authorization model.

Possible logical entries:
- `txo-auto`;
- `txo-general`;
- `txo-agent`;
- `txo-fast`;
- explicit supported logical model aliases where useful.

Provider/account names such as `chatgpt/account-a` or raw OpenRouter model identifiers must remain implementation details.

An AgentIdentity may be authorized for a set of logical models and may select among that set at runtime.

The gateway remains responsible for:
- provider credential selection;
- tenant credential pool;
- retry/cooldown/fallback;
- model/provider availability;
- accounting/usage.

## Important distinction

Do **not** encode one immutable model into the runtime profile.

Runtime profile = Hermes engine/image/security/capability ceiling.

Functional/model policy may define defaults and allowed logical models, consistent with #3851.

Agent/user-local preference can choose within the authorized set but cannot widen it.

## Required semantics

Define:
- tenant catalog of available logical model capabilities;
- per-agent authorized subset;
- default/preferred model;
- optional task-oriented aliases;
- behavior when requested model is unavailable;
- behavior when a fallback changes actual provider/model;
- whether agents can query an availability/capabilities endpoint;
- status/telemetry showing logical requested model vs resolved upstream deployment;
- compatibility with both Chat Completions and Responses API consumers.

No provider secret or account identifier in AgentIdentity.

## Dynamic availability

An agent should not need reconfiguration because one upstream credential is cooling down.

If the requested logical model is still satisfiable by another authorized deployment/credential, the proxy resolves it transparently.

If no authorized deployment remains:
- fail explicitly; or
- use an explicitly configured logical-model fallback.

Never silently fall back to an unauthorized model/provider.

## Key minting

Extend scoped model-access key generation so a key is authorized for the agent's effective logical model set, not all gateway models and not only `txo-default`.

Rotation/revocation semantics from the existing model-access contract must remain intact.

## Acceptance

- [ ] model selection is independent from provider credential ownership;
- [ ] an agent can select between at least two authorized logical models with the same Fabric virtual key;
- [ ] another unauthorized model is rejected;
- [ ] provider/account selection remains invisible to Hermes;
- [ ] model unavailability can be handled by proxy routing/fallback without leaking credentials;
- [ ] tenant-specific provider pools from the credential-pool issue are respected;
- [ ] virtual keys carry the minimum effective logical-model allowlist;
- [ ] Hindsight embedding key remains restricted to embedding routes;
- [ ] status/audit records requested logical model and resolved provider/model safely;
- [ ] tests cover authorization, fallback and fail-closed behavior.

## Authority

Roadmap v2.9 **Stage 8 — BI, AI/ML research, AI Adviser, Natural Computing & advanced intelligence**.

This issue tracks **shared AI/BI surface rationalisation** so later intelligence features do not create competing metrics, models or dashboards.

## Status

**Active from 25 October 2026.** Stage 8 has now started after Point 7 source completion. The first implementation slice is the AIIBP Data Hub foundation, extending the existing Stage-5 Marketing Data Core rather than creating a parallel ingestion system.

## Problem to solve

Current and planned surfaces include Marketing Attribution, AI Marketing Insights, revenue/demand forecasting, capacity planning, premium analytics, executive views and Management Copilot. Their maturity differs, so they should not be collapsed prematurely; however, they must consume shared sources of truth.

The Data Hub additionally provides the first-party ELT/BI capability layer needed for cross-source querying, reusable views, governed destination tasks and AI evidence. It is functionally comparable to the category served by managed data-integration products such as Windsor.ai, while remaining an original AIIBP implementation with no copied third-party code, branding or proprietary UI.

## Acceptance direction

1. define canonical BI metrics, dimensions, provenance and evidence contracts;
2. establish the governed Data Hub over Stage-5 raw/normalised lineage;
3. establish the governed Python AI service / AI Gateway and model/tool registry;
4. require AI interpretation to consume canonical operational/BI facts rather than recomputing competing truths;
5. rationalise overlapping UI only after shared contracts are stable;
6. preserve RBAC, audit, tenant boundaries, explainability and human approval;
7. evaluate ML against documented deterministic/statistical baselines before production promotion;
8. defer Natural Computing production integration until predictive/AI Adviser/baseline optimisation maturity gates are met.

## Current implementation slice

Branch: `dev1-stage8-aiibp-data-hub`

Implemented source work includes:
- tenant-scoped cross-source query/blend contract over `MarketingNormalisedRecord`;
- reusable Data Hub saved views;
- deterministic calculated marketing metrics and provenance;
- destination-task contracts and preview ledger with live execution disabled;
- AI evidence API;
- `/admin/system/data-hub` management workspace;
- dedicated Point 8 Data Hub regression/build gate.

See `docs/roadmap/stage-8-data-hub-implementation.md` for the implementation and activation boundary.

## Developer lanes

- **DEV1:** owns SalonAI reference implementation and integration.
- **DEV2:** re-engage for concrete UI/HCI/accessibility/error findings.
- **DEV3:** re-engage for concrete dashboard/RBAC/management completeness findings.
- **DEV4:** participates when capabilities are promoted into tenant-aware/shared AIIBP services.

## Primary owner
Codex/Astra as independent reviewer/test author. Do not implement Claude-owned feature code unless a follow-up issue explicitly assigns it.

## Depends on
Run incrementally after #2–#6 produce reviewable branches/PRs.

## Goal
Independently verify that Phase 1 implementation matches repository architecture and failure-safety requirements.

## Review checklist
- package dependency direction from ADR-008;
- no Prisma/Meta/React imports in `packages/domain`;
- no Meta-specific fields leaking into channel-neutral application logic;
- tenant/workspace isolation tests;
- webhook idempotency and duplicate enqueue behavior;
- BullMQ deterministic IDs and safe retry semantics;
- no blocking sleeps in webhooks;
- ambiguous-send path never blindly retries;
- secret/env validation and no committed credentials;
- TypeScript strictness and exhaustive union handling.

## Independent test additions
Prefer adding tests in separate review/test branches for uncovered invariants, especially concurrency, duplicate events, cross-workspace isolation, superseded jobs and malformed external payloads.

## Deliverable
For each reviewed PR, produce a concise review record: PASS / CHANGES_REQUESTED with exact file/line or test evidence. If a systemic architecture mismatch is found, propose an ADR/spec amendment rather than silently normalizing it in code.

# Hardening & Business Value Realization Phase

## Purpose

This phase begins from the accepted VS8 baseline on `main` at merge commit `86af939dc5782496e141cbfe90a4d11465fd4407`.

VS1–VS8 proved the core platform, identity/tenancy, governed retrieval, structured data, saved reports, actions, connectors, operations and self-hosted commercialization baseline. The next phase is not feature sprawl. It converts that technical platform into a product whose administration, governance and end-user journey match how OpenJM will actually be deployed and sold.

This issue is the **authoritative execution umbrella** for #40–#44 and the manual post-VS8 user-journey findings.

No Workspace code belongs in this phase. Workspace remains a separate product and may integrate only through the existing API boundary.

---

## Product model

### OpenJM Platform Admin
OpenJM employees/operators manage the machinery and control plane:
- tenants/accounts;
- initial customer onboarding;
- platform operators;
- identity/provider health;
- model-provider routing/status;
- system health/version;
- raw Connectors;
- raw Operations/scheduler/notification machinery;
- audited support/delegation.

Platform administration must **not automatically grant standing customer-content access**.

### Client Admin
Customer administrators manage safe business configuration:
- departments/groups;
- users/memberships;
- delegated data stewards/owners;
- access policy/classification mappings;
- tenant settings;
- simplified approved automations where later exposed.

### Data Steward / Data Owner
Authorized client personnel curate governed sources of truth:
- Knowledge documents;
- authorized Data connections;
- source metadata/classification;
- source ownership/domain;
- governed Saved Reports and later approval/curation.

A steward may be centralized IT/Technology or delegated Finance/HR/Ops/etc.

### End User
The primary experience is Chat:
- ask questions;
- receive answers and evidence the user is authorized to see;
- create real downloadable artifacts;
- optionally use explicit Chat/Knowledge/Data/Hybrid modes while they remain necessary.

The ordinary user should not be exposed to platform machinery.

---

## Guiding principle

**OpenJM Admin = machinery and control plane.**  
**Client Admin = safe, simplified business configuration.**  
**End User = clean, outcome-focused experience, primarily through Chat.**

Anything user-facing should be clean, simple and outcome-oriented. Operational complexity belongs behind admin/control-plane surfaces unless a simpler customer-facing abstraction is intentionally designed.

---

# Security and governance architecture

## 1. RBAC controls capabilities; source policy controls data

Do not solve this only by adding more global roles.

Use:
- **RBAC/capability permissions** for what a principal may do in the product;
- **source-level authorization / ABAC-style policy** for which governed information that principal may retrieve.

A person may be allowed to use Knowledge without being allowed to retrieve every Knowledge document.

The model must **never receive unauthorized evidence** and then be expected to hide it after generation.

Authorization must be applied before:
- RAG evidence enters model context;
- structured schemas/tables/rows become queryable;
- Hybrid composes evidence;
- report snapshots/exports are returned;
- artifacts expose evidence.

## 2. Source metadata/classification

Design a source-governance model capable of carrying at least:
- business domain/department;
- source owner/data steward;
- classification;
- allowed groups/roles/capabilities;
- optional explicit allow/deny principals where justified;
- sensitivity/retention tags where useful.

Default classification vocabulary should be business-friendly, e.g.:
- Public
- Internal
- Confidential
- Restricted / Highly Restricted

Allow future tenant customization; do not hard-wire governmental terminology such as "Top Secret" as the only highest tier.

Examples:
- HR employee handbook: domain=HR, classification=Internal, allowed=All Employees.
- compensation database: domain=HR/Finance, classification=Highly Restricted, allowed=explicit HR/Finance leadership groups.

## 3. Groups and departments

Client admins need:
- departments/domains;
- groups;
- group membership;
- delegated stewardship;
- mapping between groups and allowed source policies.

Avoid display-name or email-string guessing as authorization.

## 4. OpenJM support access

OpenJM must be able to perform initial setup/support if the client requests it, but support access should be:
- explicit;
- audited;
- scoped;
- preferably delegated/time-bound where practical;
- separable between tenant metadata administration and customer-content access;
- revocable without damaging tenant ownership.

## 5. Governed reports

Saved Reports are evidence-backed governed snapshots, not generic Chat files.

They:
- retain provenance;
- re-check current source authorization on open/rerun/export;
- cannot bypass revoked access;
- may later support explicit `Approved/Authoritative/Featured` curation by authorized data owners.

Merely saving a report must **not** silently train the model or alter retrieval priority.

---

# Phase execution plan

## BV1 — Authorization and information-governance foundation

This is the architectural foundation and should precede broad UI simplification.

Deliver:
- explicit platform-operator authorization separate from tenant `viewer/editor/admin/owner`;
- departments/groups;
- delegated data-steward capability;
- source metadata/classification model for Knowledge and Data;
- server-side source authorization enforcement before RAG/SQL/Hybrid/model context;
- report/export revalidation against source policy;
- audit for classification, ownership and access-policy changes;
- migrations and rollback notes;
- negative cross-tenant/cross-group tests.

Do not casually overload existing roles if capabilities/policy assignments are clearer.

### BV1 acceptance
- unauthorized source never reaches model context;
- classification/group revocation takes effect on next authorized operation or defined bounded cache window;
- Knowledge and Data both enforce equivalent policy semantics;
- Hybrid cannot use one authorized source to smuggle an unauthorized source;
- saved report/open/export/rerun re-checks current access;
- tenant isolation remains intact;
- platform operator metadata access does not imply customer-content access;
- all mutations audited.

---

## BV2 — Immediate user-journey hardening

Implement the high-confidence findings already tracked:

### #41 Data form alignment
Fix Connection URI / Engine / Display Name alignment without brittle pixel hacks.

### #42 Knowledge ingestion state
- block duplicate upload interaction during ingestion;
- clear processing state with filename/stage;
- use server lifecycle where available;
- fail/retry cleanly;
- preserve backend duplicate protection.

### #44 governed report naming
Current backend already accepts `SaveReportRequest.title`.
Frontend must:
- prompt before saving;
- suggest a sensible title;
- allow edit;
- save only after confirmation;
- preserve idempotency/evidence contract.

### Navigation correction
- remove raw Connectors and Operations from ordinary customer navigation;
- do not only hide them: backend authority must match intended admin scope;
- ordinary users should not see control-plane machinery.

### BV2 acceptance
Manual browser walkthrough plus frontend tests/typecheck/build.

---

## BV3 — Administration & provisioning control plane (#40)

Build two clearly separate scopes:

### OpenJM control plane
- tenants;
- initial tenant owner/admin provisioning;
- platform operators;
- identity configuration/status;
- private model-provider status/configuration where safe;
- raw Connectors;
- raw Operations/scheduler/notification machinery;
- health/version/audit;
- explicit support/delegation workflows.

### Client administration
- users/memberships;
- departments/groups;
- roles/capabilities;
- data stewards;
- classifications/policies;
- session revocation;
- safe tenant preferences.

Raw scheduler concepts such as operation IDs, JSON targets, misfire policy and retry internals remain OpenJM-operator tooling.

If customers need automation later, expose outcome-oriented business controls on top:
- "Refresh this source nightly"
- "Send this report every Monday"
- "Notify Finance if this fails"

Do not expose the raw machinery merely because the backend can support it.

### Bootstrap
Provide a secure, one-time-safe first-admin bootstrap:
- no default password;
- no production dev-auth fallback;
- explicit operator action;
- audited;
- cannot be reused for privilege escalation.

### Local walkthroughs
Document:
- dev-auth walkthrough for development;
- production-like local IdP + PostgreSQL walkthrough.

---

## BV4 — Chat-first customer experience (#44)

Ordinary users should primarily interact with Chat.

Knowledge, Data and governed Reports become stewardship/governance surfaces visible only to authorized users.

Do not rely on navigation hiding for security.

Explicit modes `Chat / Knowledge / Data / Hybrid` may remain visible for now.

### Important deferral: automatic natural-language routing

The desired end-state is that ordinary users do not have to choose a mode.

However, **removing the mode selector is not a priority for this phase**.

The current explicit modes are acceptable and commercially useful.

Do not remove them until implicit routing can be proven with a high acceptance bar:
- representative real-world queries route correctly;
- no silent evidence loss;
- no unauthorized source access;
- predictable fallback/abstention;
- user can understand which evidence/tools were used.

Until then, explicit modes remain a safe advanced/user control.

---

## BV5 — Chat artifacts (#43)

General Chat must create real downloadable files when requested instead of printing file source into the transcript.

Initial formats:
- HTML;
- PDF;
- DOCX;
- Markdown/TXT;
- CSV for appropriate tabular results.

XLSX can follow when justified.

Artifact requirements:
- persisted artifact metadata;
- conversation/message linkage;
- safe filenames/MIME allow-list;
- tenant/user authorization on download;
- bounded size;
- no path traversal;
- no arbitrary filesystem/shell/network execution;
- controlled server renderers;
- retention/deletion policy.

Evidence-backed artifact:
- preserve citations/as-of provenance.

General Chat artifact:
- must not be falsely represented as a governed/approved report.

Keep **Chat Artifact** and **Governed Saved Report** as separate product concepts.

---

## BV6 — Governed business-value layer

Once the source policy and stewardship model are stable, improve the Reports governance role.

Consider:
- Approved / Authoritative / Featured report state;
- data-owner approval workflow;
- explicit curation metadata;
- controlled use of approved reports as preferred retrieval candidates where policy allows;
- departmental ownership.

Do **not** make every saved report automatically authoritative or alter retrieval ranking merely because it was saved.

This can be a later sub-phase if BV1–BV5 are already substantial.

---

# Known issue mapping

- #40 Administration & provisioning portal
- #41 Data source form alignment
- #42 Knowledge ingestion UX
- #43 Chat downloadable artifacts
- #44 Chat-first UX / Knowledge-Data-Reports stewardship
- #9 Workspace integration boundary — remains independent and must not be contaminated by this phase

---

# Hermes execution rules

1. Base all implementation on current `main` at or after `86af939dc5782496e141cbfe90a4d11465fd4407`.
2. Do not merge without explicit human approval.
3. Do not start Workspace implementation or share product internals with Workspace.
4. Begin with a short architecture/reconciliation pass against existing VS5–VS8 identity, permission, RAG, structured-query, reports and connector code.
5. Reuse existing primitives where safe; do not rebuild accepted systems unnecessarily.
6. Security/authorization changes require RED→GREEN tests before UI wiring.
7. Keep migrations additive, versioned and rollback-aware.
8. Batch local commands/tests; use GitHub Actions for full regression; inspect failing logs rather than repeatedly rerunning green suites.
9. Keep PRs reviewable and bounded. Prefer coherent vertical increments, not one giant all-phase PR.
10. Preserve fail-closed behavior and evidence-or-abstain semantics.
11. Never hide a security weakness behind UI visibility.
12. No unsupported PASS claims. Mark NOT RUN explicitly.
13. Stop at each merge boundary for review.

## Suggested PR sequence

- **BV1-A:** platform-operator + group/department/data-steward authorization foundation.
- **BV1-B:** Knowledge source classification + pre-retrieval enforcement.
- **BV1-C:** Data/structured source classification + SQL/Hybrid enforcement + report authorization propagation.
- **BV2:** immediate UI/user-journey corrections (#41/#42/report naming/navigation).
- **BV3-A:** OpenJM control-plane APIs + secure bootstrap.
- **BV3-B:** client-admin APIs + admin UX.
- **BV3-C:** Connectors/Operations relocation and authorization reconciliation.
- **BV4:** Chat-first permission-aware customer navigation.
- **BV5-A:** artifact model/storage/API + HTML.
- **BV5-B:** PDF/DOCX and evidence-preserving artifact rendering.
- **BV6:** governed report curation only after previous gates are accepted.

Parallelize only work that cannot change the same authorization/data contracts underneath another active PR.

---

# Global phase acceptance

Before declaring this phase complete, prove:

### End user
- can primarily use Chat;
- sees only authorized evidence;
- can create real downloadable artifacts;
- does not need to understand control-plane machinery.

### Data steward
- can manage only authorized governed sources;
- can classify/tag sources;
- can create/name governed reports;
- source/report access follows group/classification policy.

### Client admin
- can manage users/groups/stewards/policies without platform-level access;
- cannot cross tenant boundary;
- can revoke access and have it take effect promptly.

### OpenJM admin
- can onboard/manage tenants and platform machinery;
- can support initial setup through explicit audited authority;
- does not receive implicit blanket customer-content access.

### Security
- negative tenant/group/classification tests;
- unauthorized evidence never enters model context;
- report/artifact downloads enforce current authorization;
- no secret leakage;
- no production dev-auth fallback;
- audit coverage for privileged mutations.

### Regression
- accepted VS1–VS8 behavior remains green;
- frontend tests/typecheck/build green;
- exact-head CI acceptance before merge;
- manual browser journey repeated at the end of the phase.

---

# Definition of success

The phase is successful when OpenJM no longer feels like a set of technically capable VS1–VS8 surfaces exposed equally to everyone.

It should behave as a commercial enterprise product:
- OpenJM operates the platform;
- client admins govern their organization;
- data owners govern approved sources of truth;
- access follows the user and the data classification;
- ordinary employees mainly ask questions in Chat;
- OpenJM returns only authorized evidence and useful downloadable work products;
- operational complexity stays behind the appropriate admin boundary.

# Nexus Delivery Governance v5

```yaml
protocol_id: nexus-delivery-gates-v5
protocol_version: 5.0.0
effective_at: 2026-07-27T00:00:00Z
repository: Maximvonshaft/nexus_helpdesk
authority_issue: 722
execution_model: EVENT_DRIVEN
root_cause_model: ONE_FINDING_ONE_WORK_ITEM
production_release_authority: false
destructive_retirement_authority: false
default_posture: NO_GO
status: CURRENT_STABLE_PROTOCOL
```

## 1. Purpose

This Issue defines the stable delivery protocol only. It must not name a mutable current branch, Pull Request, commit SHA, workflow run, candidate status or production status.

Current facts belong in the work item that owns them:

- full production activation authority: #533;
- Phase 1 WebChat deployment and target qualification: #709;
- WebChat target business acceptance: #843;
- repository settings and branch protection: #179;
- recovery/restore/retention/DSAR evidence: #532;
- performance/capacity/SLO evidence: #757;
- capability-specific Provider qualification: the corresponding stable capability Issue.

Machine-readable authorities:

- delivery gates: `config/governance/delivery-gates.v1.json`;
- repository authority and retirement inventory: `docs/ai/codebase-rationalization-inventory.v2.yaml`;
- repository acceptance: `.github/workflows/canonical-acceptance.yml`;
- downstream immutable candidate convergence: `.github/workflows/controlled-candidate-convergence.yml`.

## 2. Event-driven execution

Governance runs only when a material fact changes:

- Pull Request code, Head, review or `required-gate` state;
- `main` identity;
- selected image, configuration, Alembic head or environment identity;
- production incident state;
- production business metric crossing an accepted threshold.

No fact change means no repeated whole-repository scan, duplicate Finding, status comment or new governance artifact.

## 3. Five gates

### Gate 1 — Business / Product

Required evidence:

- one `journey_key`;
- accountable business owner;
- customer-visible terminal outcome;
- metric, denominator and target;
- failure recovery path.

Do not create platform abstractions or a second product surface without a current user/business need.

### Gate 2 — Architecture / Data

Required evidence:

- one Aggregate and one writer for each mutable fact;
- Tenant authority;
- migration and rollback boundaries;
- idempotency and concurrency contract;
- deletion evidence for the superseded path.

Parallel writers, dual truth and compatibility without a bounded retirement contract are forbidden.

### Gate 3 — Security / Privacy

Required evidence:

- server-side capability authorization;
- Tenant scope;
- data classification and retention;
- secret, dependency, SAST and CodeQL evidence;
- DSAR, Legal Hold and lifecycle impact.

### Gate 4 — Release / Runtime

All evidence must bind one immutable candidate:

- source SHA and tree SHA;
- image digest;
- Alembic head;
- configuration identity;
- rollback/recovery identity;
- `/healthz`, `/readyz` and required Worker progress.

A PR body, historical run, mutable tag or verbal statement is not candidate evidence.

### Gate 5 — Production Outcome

Required evidence:

- bounded activation or Provider canary;
- business metric, denominator, target and trend;
- incident owner and kill switch;
- customer-visible terminal outcome;
- accountable GO/NO-GO decision bound to the same SHA, image, configuration and environment.

Green CI is not production GO. Technical success is not business completion.

## 4. One root cause, one work item

A root cause has one stable `root_cause_key` and one primary work item. Other gates may attach evidence, but they must not create a duplicate Finding or parallel implementation PR.

Minimum Finding contract:

```yaml
finding_id:
root_cause_key:
classification:
severity:
confidence:
bound_sha_or_head:
evidence:
current_behavior:
expected_contract:
impact:
owner:
acceptance_evidence_required:
external_evidence_required:
```

## 5. State model

```text
implementation_state:
  NOT_OBSERVED | PATCHED | MERGED

verification_state:
  NOT_VERIFIED | LOCAL_VERIFIED | CI_VERIFIED | INDEPENDENTLY_REVIEWED

release_state:
  NOT_CANDIDATE | CANDIDATE | CONTROLLED | PROVIDER_CANARY | FULL

production_state:
  NOT_VERIFIED | HEALTH_VERIFIED | BUSINESS_OUTCOME_VERIFIED
```

Completion of one state or gate never promotes the next state automatically.

## 6. Allowed work

The active business scope is governed by `config/business/golden-journeys.v1.json` and `docs/operations/90-day-scope-freeze.md`.

Allowed work is limited to:

1. closing missing acceptance for an approved Golden Journey;
2. fixing a P0 customer, security, privacy, data-integrity or production incident root cause;
3. deleting duplicate implementation, stale authority or retired path that obstructs the first two items.

## 7. Safety boundary

This Issue does not authorize:

- merge;
- deployment;
- Provider traffic;
- production GO;
- production data deletion;
- destructive retirement.

Those actions require the exact owning authority, immutable evidence and explicit action decision. Current execution status must be recorded only in the relevant stable work item, not by mutating this protocol into a status ledger.

## Canonical program — no new lifecycle

Parent operation: `marketontology-complete-parity-fanout-20260826-sol-001`  
Canonical owners: `WS:MARKET-OS` + shared semantic owner `WS:ALPHA-INTELLIGENCE-INTEGRATION`  
Existing durable allocation manifest: `agentos/handoffs/MARKET-ONTOLOGY-F00-F13-FABLE-COO-FANOUT-MANIFEST-2026-08-26.md`  
Existing original Slack parent: `C0BSBM78V1N/1787803489.738949`

This issue is a current GitHub coordination/proof projection for the existing complete-parity program. It creates no Executive Job, Attempt, Worker, queue, workstream, authority plane, receiver assignment, branch, deployment, or product effect.

## Current Chairman outcome

Build out the complete lawful Market Ontology capability space into Mastermind, not merely a curated subset. Priority controls sequencing, not inclusion. Preserve every admitted capability/job with source-row traceability and one explicit disposition: stronger existing equivalent, upgrade, original implementation, contextual projection, rights-gated hold, or exact `REJECTED_BY_DESIGN` ruling.

The target is not a visual clone or copying proprietary code, text, data, assets, or branding. The target is independently implemented capability parity and then a Mastermind-native integration/synergy phase using first-party, public, licensed, or otherwise lawful data.

Completion remains three distinct milestones:

1. `COVERAGE_COMPLETE` — every admitted row is owned and dispositioned with no unknowns.
2. `PARITY_COMPLETE` — every lawful row has a working equivalent or adjudicated impossibility, with real consumers and proof.
3. `BEYOND_PARITY_RATCHET_ACTIVE` — the absorbed capabilities are integrated with Mastermind intelligence, portfolio, research, distribution, and learning systems so the combined product is stronger than either source system alone.

