## Design

### Constants and types

```rust
/// Idle connections retained per peer key. A total across the whole process, not per worker.
pub const DEFAULT_MAX_IDLE_PER_PEER: usize = 32;
/// Hard ceiling on idle connections per peer.
pub const MAX_IDLE_PER_PEER_LIMIT: usize = 1_024;
/// Default idle timeout in coarse milliseconds.
pub const DEFAULT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS: u32 = 60_000;
/// Default number of connections `prewarm` opens for a peer. Zero means prewarming is off,
/// which is the right default: opening connections to an endpoint that may never receive
/// traffic costs the upstream a socket for nothing, and the feature exists for
/// latency-critical deployments with stable membership rather than for everyone.
pub const DEFAULT_MIN_CONNS: usize = 0;
/// Expired connections a single checkout may discard before giving up and returning `None`.
pub const CHECKOUT_EXPIRY_BUDGET: u32 = 8;
/// Peers a single `evict_idle` sweep may visit.
pub const EVICT_SWEEP_BUDGET: usize = 256;

/// Upstream scheme, which is part of the pool key because a plaintext and a TLS connection
/// to the same address are not interchangeable.
#[derive(Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Debug)]
pub enum PeerScheme { Http, Https }

/// Negotiated-protocol preference, part of the key because a request needing HTTP/1.1 must
/// not be handed an HTTP/2 connection. This is the field whose absence causes Pingora's
/// `PreferredHttpVersion` defect.
#[derive(Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Debug)]
pub enum AlpnPref { H1Only, H2Only, H2ThenH1 }

/// Identity of a configured upstream client certificate. Assigned densely by the TLS
/// configuration compiler; `None` means no client certificate is presented.
#[derive(Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Debug, PartialOrd, Ord)]
pub struct CertId(pub u32);

/// Identity of a configured upstream verification profile (trust anchors, verification mode,
/// revocation settings). Assigned densely by the TLS configuration compiler.
#[derive(Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Debug, PartialOrd, Ord)]
pub struct VerifyProfileId(pub u32);

/// Everything that must match for two upstream connections to be interchangeable.
///
/// This is the pool key. It is compared by full structural equality on every pool hit; the
/// hash is only an index. Adding a field here is a correctness change, not a refactor: a
/// field that distinguishes two connections and is absent from this struct is a cross-tenant
/// leak.
#[derive(Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Debug)]
pub struct PeerKey {
    pub endpoint: EndpointId,
    pub addr: EndpointAddr,
    pub scheme: PeerScheme,
    pub sni: Option<Box<str>>,
    pub alpn: AlpnPref,
    pub client_cert: Option<CertId>,
    pub verify: VerifyProfileId,
    /// Authority of an upstream HTTP CONNECT proxy, when one is configured.
    pub proxy: Option<Box<str>>,
}

/// One pooled connection plus the metadata the pool needs about it.
struct IdleConn<C> {
    conn: C,
    /// When it was returned to the pool.
    idle_since_ms: CoarseMillis,
}

/// Immutable index from `PeerKey` to a pool slot. Rebuilt when the configured peer set
/// changes, which is configuration rate, not request rate.
///
/// Parallel arrays, for the same reason the ketama ring uses them: the binary search reads
/// only `hashes`, so 8 candidates share a 64-byte line. An array of
/// `(u64, PeerKey, u32)` structs would be roughly 104 bytes per entry, so a 10000-peer
/// index would be about 1 MB and a 14-level search would be 14 misses over that megabyte,
/// which is 150 to 200 ns and misses this issue's own 60 ns checkout budget. With a
/// separate `hashes` array the search touches 80 KB.
pub struct PeerIndex {
    /// Ascending, one per peer. The only array the binary search reads.
    hashes: Box<[u64]>,
    /// The full key for each entry, in the same order. Read once, at the end, to verify.
    keys: Box<[PeerKey]>,
    /// The pool slot for each entry, in the same order.
    slots: Box<[u32]>,
}

/// One published generation of the pool: the index and the slot array it numbers.
///
/// These are ONE object behind ONE `ArcSwap` and must never be split into two. If a worker
/// could load a new index and an old slot array, it would resolve a key to a slot number
/// assigned by the new index and then index the old array with it, handing back a
/// connection pooled under a completely different peer key. A bounds check does not catch
/// that: the slot is in range, it is simply the wrong peer. That is precisely the
/// cross-tenant leak this whole issue exists to prevent, reintroduced through the back door.
struct PoolGeneration<C> {
    /// Monotonically increasing, assigned by `rebuild`. A checked-out connection carries the
    /// id of the generation whose slot numbering it was issued under, and `checkin` closes
    /// the connection rather than pooling it when the ids differ. Without the stamp, a
    /// connection checked out before a rebuild and returned after it would be pushed into
    /// whatever peer now owns that slot number.
    gen_id: u64,
    index: PeerIndex,
    /// One idle queue per slot, allocated when the generation is built. Slots are stable
    /// for the life of a generation.
    slots: Box<[SlotState<C>]>,
}

/// The process-wide upstream connection pool.
pub struct ConnPool<C: Send> {
    /// The index and the slots as one atomically swapped unit.
    state: arc_swap::ArcSwap<PoolGeneration<C>>,
    /// Rotating start position for `evict_idle`, so successive sweeps cover the pool.
    evict_cursor: core::sync::atomic::AtomicUsize,
    /// Checkouts whose key was absent from the index. A lossy diagnostic counter, so a
    /// relaxed `fetch_add` on a line nobody reads per request is the right cost. It is an
    /// atomic and not a plain field because `checkout` takes `&self`.
    unpoolable: core::sync::atomic::AtomicU64,
    registry: &'static EndpointRegistry,
    cfg: PoolCfg,
}

struct SlotState<C> {
    idle: crossbeam_queue::ArrayQueue<IdleConn<C>>,
}

/// A checked-out connection. Releases the `active_conns` balance on drop and returns the
/// connection to the pool if `checkin` is called explicitly.
#[must_use = "dropping this closes the connection instead of pooling it"]
pub struct PooledConn<'a, C: Send> {
    conn: Option<C>,
    slot: u32,
    /// The generation whose slot numbering `slot` refers to.
    gen_id: u64,
    pool: &'a ConnPool<C>,
    guard: ConnGuard<'a>,
}
```

### Algorithm: hashing and lookup

`peer_hash` is **keyed SipHash-1-3** over a canonical byte rendering of the fields in declaration
order, written out so that two implementations cannot differ. The 128-bit key is drawn once per
process from the entropy source and stored on the `ConnPool`.

**Why keyed and not FNV-1a, given that the full key is compared anyway.** The full comparison makes a
collision *safe*; it does not make a collision *cheap*. `lookup` walks forward over every entry
sharing a hash, so `c` colliding keys make every checkout for any of them `O(c)`. FNV-1a is a public
unkeyed function over bytes that include the SNI and the proxy authority, and in a multi-tenant
gateway those strings come from tenant-supplied configuration. A tenant who can name their own
upstream hostname can compute thousands of names that hash to the same value offline and land them all
in one bucket, turning every checkout in the process into a linear scan. That is a control-plane
algorithmic-complexity attack on the request path, and it costs nothing to close.

The stability requirement is what makes keying free: the hash is an in-process index only, so it
**must not** be stable across processes and there is nothing to preserve. Note the contrast with
`membership_hash` in `cluster-snapshot-and-eligible-slices` (#71), which is also unkeyed FNV-1a: that
one is guarded by an exact comparison of the whole membership before it is trusted, and it is never
walked over. Same function, different obligations.

```rust
/// Per-process SipHash key for the pool index. Drawn once from the entropy source in
/// `ConnPool::new` and never derived from configuration: the pool is process-local, so
/// cross-process stability is not required and unpredictability is.
struct PoolHashKey(u64, u64);

fn peer_hash(k: &PoolHashKey, key: &PeerKey) -> u64 {
    use core::hash::Hasher;
    let mut h = siphasher::sip::SipHasher13::new_with_keys(k.0, k.1);
    // The same canonical byte sequence as below, fed through `h.write(..)` instead of `fnv`.
    // Every field, every discriminant byte and every separator byte is written in the same
    // order; only the mixing function changes.
    ...
    h.finish()
}
```

The canonical byte sequence, which is what the separators and discriminants define and which does not
change:

```rust
fn peer_bytes(key: &PeerKey, h: &mut impl FnMut(&[u8])) {
    h(&key.endpoint.0.to_le_bytes());
    match &key.addr {
        EndpointAddr::Socket(sa) => {
            h(&[0u8]);
            match sa.ip() {
                core::net::IpAddr::V4(a) => { h(&[4u8]); h(&a.octets()); }
                core::net::IpAddr::V6(a) => { h(&[6u8]); h(&a.octets()); }
            }
            h(&sa.port().to_le_bytes());
        }
        EndpointAddr::Unix(p) => {
            h(&[1u8]);
            h(p.as_os_str().as_encoded_bytes());
        }
    }
    h(&[key.scheme as u8, key.alpn as u8]);
    h(&[2u8]);
    if let Some(s) = &key.sni { h(s.as_bytes()); }
    h(&[3u8]);
    h(&key.client_cert.map_or(u32::MAX, |c| c.0).to_le_bytes());
    h(&key.verify.0.to_le_bytes());
    h(&[4u8]);
    if let Some(p) = &key.proxy { h(p.as_bytes()); }
}
```

The separator bytes are what stop `sni = Some("ab")`, `proxy = None` from hashing equal to
`sni = Some("a")`, `proxy = Some("b")`. `PeerScheme` and `AlpnPref` need
`#[repr(u8)]` and a `Copy` bound for the `as u8` casts.

```
lookup(index, key) -> Option<u32>
 1. let h = peer_hash(key)
 2. let mut i = index.hashes.partition_point(|&x| x < h)   // FIRST position with hashes[i] >= h
                                                           // `binary_search` returns an ARBITRARY
                                                           // matching position when duplicates
                                                           // exist and must not be used here
 3. while index.hashes.get(i) == Some(&h) {
        if index.keys.get(i)? == key { return index.slots.get(i).copied() }  // FULL comparison
        i += 1;
    }
 4. return None                                           // unknown peer: not poolable
```

Step 3's comparison is the whole correction. It compares every field, including the `Option<Box<str>>`
SNI and proxy, using the derived `PartialEq`. In the common case the hash matches exactly one entry, so it
is one comparison; the `Box<str>` comparisons are pointer-length-then-bytes and the strings are short.

### Algorithm: checkout

```
checkout(&self, key, now_ms) -> Checkout<'_, C>
 1. let gen = self.state.load()                           // ONE load: index and slots together
 2. let Some(slot) = lookup(&gen.index, key) else {
        self.unpoolable.fetch_add(1, Relaxed)             // lossy diagnostic, never read per request
        return Checkout::NotPoolable
    }
 3. let Some(state) = gen.slots.get(slot as usize) else {
        debug_assert!(false, "index and slots are one object; a slot is always in range");
        return Checkout::NotPoolable
    }
 4. let Some(stats) = self.registry.stats(key.endpoint) else {
        return Checkout::NotPoolable                      // endpoint retired: do not pool
    }
 5. for _ in 0..CHECKOUT_EXPIRY_BUDGET:
 6.     let Some(idle) = state.idle.pop() else { return Checkout::Miss { slot, gen_id: gen.gen_id } }
 7.     if now_ms.wrapping_sub(idle.idle_since_ms) >= self.cfg.idle_timeout_ms {
            drop(idle.conn)                               // closes the socket
            continue
        }
 8.     let guard = ConnGuard::acquire(stats)
 9.     return Checkout::Hit(PooledConn { conn: Some(idle.conn), slot,
                                          gen_id: gen.gen_id, pool: self, guard })
10. return Checkout::Miss { slot, gen_id: gen.gen_id }                        // budget exhausted: dial a new one
```

The expiry budget bounds the work: a pool full of stale connections cannot make one checkout walk the
whole queue. Discarded connections are dropped, which closes them, and the `ConnGuard` they were holding
was already released when they were checked in.

### Algorithm: checkin

```
checkin(mut conn: PooledConn<'_, C>, now_ms)
 1. let Some(c) = conn.conn.take() else { return }          // already consumed
 2. let gen = conn.pool.state.load()
 3. if gen.gen_id != conn.gen_id { drop(c); return }
    // A slot number is only meaningful within the generation that assigned it. After a
    // rebuild the same number belongs to a different peer, so pooling into it would hand
    // this connection to that peer on a later checkout. Close it instead; the cost is one
    // connection per in-flight request per rebuild.
 4. let Some(state) = gen.slots.get(conn.slot as usize) else { drop(c); return }
 5. match state.idle.push(IdleConn { conn: c, idle_since_ms: now_ms }) {
        Ok(())        => {}                                // pooled
        Err(returned) => drop(returned.conn)                // queue full: close it
    }
 6. // `conn`'s Drop releases the ConnGuard, which decrements `active_conns`
```

`ArrayQueue::push` returns the value on failure rather than blocking or allocating, so a full queue closes
the connection instead of growing memory. That is the correct behaviour: `max_idle_per_peer` is a hard
total.

`PooledConn::drop` closes the connection when `checkin` was not called, which makes "forgot to return it"
safe rather than a leak, and releases the `ConnGuard` in both paths.

### Algorithm: rebuilding the index

```
rebuild(&self, keys: Vec<PeerKey>, now_ms)
 1. dedupe `keys` and sort by (hash, key)
 2. build `PeerIndex` (the three parallel arrays) with slot numbers assigned in sorted order
 3. build a new `Box<[SlotState<C>]>` of the same length. For each new slot whose key exists
    in the OLD generation's index, MOVE the old slot's `ArrayQueue` contents into the new one
    by popping and pushing, up to the new capacity; drop any excess, and drop any connection
    whose `now_ms.wrapping_sub(idle_since_ms) >= cfg.idle_timeout_ms` rather than carrying an
    already-expired connection into the new generation. This is the only use of `now_ms` in
    `rebuild`, and it is why the parameter exists. For each new key, allocate an empty queue.
 4. store ONE new `PoolGeneration { gen_id: previous + 1, index, slots }` with a single
    `ArcSwap::store`
 5. drop the old generation, which closes every connection that was not migrated
```

Step 3 is what preserves warm connections across a configuration change. Without it, every configuration
update drops every warm upstream connection, and under a churning control plane the proxy never has a warm
connection at all.

Step 4 being **one** store of **one** object is a correctness requirement, not tidiness. Two separate
swaps, one for the index and one for the slots, admit a worker that reads the new index and the old slot
array: it resolves its key to a slot number from the new numbering and then reads that number out of the
old array, which is a different peer's queue. The slot is in range, so no bounds check fires, and the
worker is handed an established, possibly mTLS-authenticated connection to the wrong upstream. That is the
exact defect this issue was written to correct, so the structure must make it unrepresentable rather than
relying on a check.

### Algorithm: eviction sweep

```
evict_idle(&self, now_ms) -> usize
 1. let gen = self.state.load(); let slots = &gen.slots
    if slots.is_empty() { return 0 }
 2. let start = self.evict_cursor.fetch_add(EVICT_SWEEP_BUDGET, Relaxed) % slots.len()
 3. let mut closed = 0
 4. for i in 0..EVICT_SWEEP_BUDGET.min(slots.len()):
       let s = &slots[(start + i) % slots.len()]
       // pop, test, and push back anything still fresh, bounded by the queue capacity
       let n = s.idle.len()
       for _ in 0..n {
           let Some(c) = s.idle.pop() else { break }
           if now_ms.wrapping_sub(c.idle_since_ms) >= self.cfg.idle_timeout_ms {
               drop(c.conn); closed += 1
           } else if s.idle.push(c).is_err() { closed += 1 }
       }
 5. return closed
```

The sweep is bounded in peers per call and in pops per peer, and the rotating cursor means successive calls
cover the whole pool. Correctness does not depend on the sweep running at all, because `checkout` also
discards expired connections; the sweep exists so that a peer receiving no traffic does not hold sockets
open indefinitely.

### The threat-model section this issue adds

`docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` gains a section titled **"Upstream connection pool"** stating, in this order:

1. What the pool holds: established, frequently mTLS-authenticated connections to upstreams belonging
   to different tenants, in one process-shared structure. It is the highest-value object in the data
   plane, because handing one to the wrong requester is a cross-tenant authentication bypass rather
   than a performance bug.
2. The defect being corrected: a pool keyed by a hash whose original key is never stored (Pingora's
   `GroupKey = u64`) hands a request another tenant's authenticated connection on a hash collision,
   silently and cumulatively over a process lifetime.
3. The defence: the full `PeerKey` is compared structurally on every hit, the index and the slot array
   are swapped as one object so no reader can pair a new slot number with an old array, and a
   checked-out connection carries the generation id it was issued under.
4. The list of fields that make two connections non-interchangeable, and the statement that adding a
   field to `PeerKey` is a correctness change: a distinguishing property absent from the key is a
   leak.
5. The hash is keyed per process specifically so that tenant-supplied SNI and proxy strings cannot be
   ground into one bucket to make every checkout a linear scan.
6. `NotPoolable` is a handshake amplifier, and request-derived values must be resolved against the
   configured key set before they reach a `PeerKey`.
7. The residual: `active_conns` counts checked-out connections and not idle ones, so any
   maximum-connection limit expressed over it is exceeded by up to `max_idle_per_peer` per peer.

### Complexity

Variables: `P` distinct peer keys, `I` idle connections per peer, `E` the expiry budget.

| Operation | Average | Worst case | Space |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `peer_hash` | O(key length) | O(key length) | 0 |
| `lookup` | O(log P) over the `8P`-byte hash array plus 1 full comparison | O(log P + collisions) | 0 |
| `checkout` hit | O(log P + 1) | O(log P + E) | 0 |
| `checkout` miss | O(log P) | O(log P) | 0 |
| `checkin` | O(1) | O(1) | 0 |
| `rebuild` | O(P log P + total idle) | same | `O(P * max_idle)` slots |
| `evict_idle` | O(EVICT_SWEEP_BUDGET * I) | same | 0 |

Retained space: one `ArrayQueue` of `max_idle_per_peer` entries per peer. At 1000 peers and 32 idle each
with a 64-byte `C`, that is about 2 MB of queue storage plus whatever the connections themselves hold.

### Invariants

- **I-CP1.** A connection is only ever handed back for a `PeerKey` that compares **fully equal** to the one
  it was pooled under. A hash match alone never suffices.
- **I-CP2.** `active_conns` is incremented exactly once per checkout, in `ConnGuard::acquire`, and
  decremented exactly once, in `ConnGuard::drop`. There is no other path.
  **It therefore counts connections that are checked out, not connections that are open.** An idle
  pooled connection holds an open socket and no guard, so the real socket count for a peer is
  `active_conns + idle_len`, and any ceiling expressed over `active_conns` alone is exceeded by up to
  `max_idle_per_peer`. State this on the field's doc comment where it is defined and wherever a limit
  is derived from it; the number that bounds file descriptors is the sum, not the counter.
- **I-CP3.** The number of idle connections for a peer never exceeds `max_idle_per_peer`.
- **I-CP4.** `checkout` performs at most `CHECKOUT_EXPIRY_BUDGET` discards before returning.
- **I-CP5.** No allocation occurs in `checkout` or `checkin`.
- **I-CP6.** No lock is taken anywhere in this module, and `unsafe` does not appear.
- **I-CP7.** A `PeerKey` not present in the index is never pooled and never collides with one that is.
- **I-CP8.** The peer index and the slot array are swapped as one object, so no reader can pair a slot
  number with a slot array from a different generation. A `PooledConn` additionally carries the
  `gen_id` it was issued under and is closed rather than pooled if it is returned after a rebuild.

### Why this design and not the obvious alternative

The obvious alternative is `DashMap<u64, ...>` keyed by a reuse hash, which is Pingora's design and is
simpler. It is rejected because the failure mode is silent cross-tenant request leakage, and because a
concurrent map is a set of sharded locks, which this crate may not contain. The second alternative is a
per-worker pool, which is NGINX's design, rejected because it multiplies idle connections by the worker
count, cold-starts every worker independently, and produces a tail-latency skew that no configuration can
fix.

## Public API

```rust
/// Pool configuration.
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct PoolCfg {
    pub max_idle_per_peer: usize,
    pub idle_timeout_ms: u32,
    /// Connections `prewarm` opens for one peer. Clamped to `max_idle_per_peer` at
    /// construction, because a prewarm that immediately overflows the idle queue would dial
    /// and then close, which is the cost of the feature with none of the benefit. Zero
    /// disables prewarming.
    pub min_conns: usize,
}

impl Default for PoolCfg {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            max_idle_per_peer: DEFAULT_MAX_IDLE_PER_PEER,
            idle_timeout_ms: DEFAULT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS,
            min_conns: DEFAULT_MIN_CONNS,
        }
    }
}

/// Result of a checkout attempt.
pub enum Checkout<'a, C: Send> {
    /// A warm connection, already accounted in `active_conns`.
    Hit(PooledConn<'a, C>),
    /// No warm connection for a known peer. The caller dials and then calls `adopt`,
    /// passing both values back unchanged so `adopt` can reject a slot from a generation
    /// that has since been replaced.
    Miss { slot: u32, gen_id: u64 },
    /// This peer key is not in the configured index, so it is not poolable. The caller dials
    /// a one-shot connection and closes it when done.
    NotPoolable,
}

impl<C: Send> ConnPool<C> {
    /// Creates an empty pool with no peers. Call `rebuild` to populate the index.
    pub fn new(registry: &'static EndpointRegistry, cfg: PoolCfg) -> Self;

    /// Replaces the peer index, migrating idle connections for peers present in both the old
    /// and the new index. Runs on the configuration plane and allocates.
    pub fn rebuild(&self, keys: Vec<PeerKey>, now_ms: CoarseMillis);

    /// Takes a warm connection for `key`, or reports why it could not.
    ///
    /// Allocation-free. Never blocks. The full key is compared before any connection is
    /// returned.
    pub fn checkout(&self, key: &PeerKey, now_ms: CoarseMillis) -> Checkout<'_, C>;

    /// Wraps a freshly dialled connection so that it is accounted and can be pooled.
    /// `slot` and `gen_id` come from `Checkout::Miss` and are returned unchanged. Returns
    /// `None` when `gen_id` is no longer the current generation, when `slot` is out of range,
    /// or when `endpoint` is no longer in the registry, in which case the caller still owns
    /// the connection and closes it. On `None` no `ConnGuard` is acquired, so nothing leaks.
    pub fn adopt(
        &self,
        slot: u32,
        gen_id: u64,
        endpoint: EndpointId,
        conn: C,
    ) -> Option<PooledConn<'_, C>>;

    /// Returns a connection to the pool. Closes it if the peer's queue is full.
    pub fn checkin(&self, conn: PooledConn<'_, C>, now_ms: CoarseMillis);

    /// Closes expired idle connections for up to `EVICT_SWEEP_BUDGET` peers, rotating through
    /// the pool across calls. Returns how many were closed.
    pub fn evict_idle(&self, now_ms: CoarseMillis) -> usize;

    /// Fills `key`'s idle queue up to `cfg.min_conns` by calling `dial`, so an endpoint that
    /// has just become healthy holds warm connections before it receives request traffic.
    /// Returns how many were pooled.
    ///
    /// This module dials nothing: it stores opaque values of type `C`. The caller supplies
    /// `dial`, which produces one ready connection or `None`, and `prewarm` stops at the
    /// first `None`, at `cfg.min_conns`, when the peer's idle queue is full, or immediately
    /// when the key is not in the index. It acquires no `ConnGuard`, because a prewarmed
    /// connection is idle rather than checked out; `active_conns` is therefore unchanged and
    /// `idle_len` rises.
    ///
    /// Runs on the caller's control-plane task and is never called from the request path.
    /// The bound on the work is `cfg.min_conns` calls to `dial`, which is why `min_conns` is
    /// clamped to `max_idle_per_peer` at construction.
    pub fn prewarm(
        &self,
        key: &PeerKey,
        now_ms: CoarseMillis,
        dial: &mut dyn FnMut() -> Option<C>,
    ) -> usize;

    /// Idle connection count for one peer. For tests and metrics.
    pub fn idle_len(&self, key: &PeerKey) -> Option<usize>;

    /// Number of peers in the index.
    pub fn peer_count(&self) -> usize;

    /// Count of checkouts that found no index entry. Diagnostic for dynamic-SNI setups.
    pub fn unpoolable_count(&self) -> u64;
}

impl<'a, C: Send> PooledConn<'a, C> {
    /// The connection. Panics never; returns `None` after the value has been taken.
    pub fn get_mut(&mut self) -> Option<&mut C>;
    /// Takes the connection out, so `checkin` will not pool it. Used when the connection is
    /// being handed to something that owns it for good, or when it must be closed.
    pub fn take(&mut self) -> Option<C>;
}
```

## Edge cases

1. **Two distinct `PeerKey`s whose hashes collide**: both are in the index under the same hash, the
   forward walk finds the right one by full comparison, and neither can receive the other's connection.
   Tested with a hand-forged collision by injecting two keys whose hashes are made equal through a
   test-only hash override (I-CP1). This test is the reason the issue exists and must not be deleted.
2. **A `PeerKey` differing only in `sni`**: distinct keys, distinct slots, no sharing. Same for
   `client_cert`, `verify`, `alpn`, `scheme` and `proxy`. One test per field, six tests, because each
   field's absence from the key is its own leak.
3. **A `PeerKey` not in the index** (dynamic SNI): `NotPoolable`, `unpoolable_count` increments, and the
   caller dials a one-shot. Assert that repeated `NotPoolable` checkouts allocate nothing and grow no
   structure.
4. **Empty pool**: `checkout` returns `Miss` for a known peer and `NotPoolable` for an unknown one. Never
   panics on an empty `slots` array.
5. **Queue full on checkin**: the connection is dropped and closed. `idle_len` stays at
   `max_idle_per_peer` (I-CP3).
6. **`max_idle_per_peer == 0`**: `ArrayQueue::new(0)` panics in `crossbeam-queue`, so clamp the capacity to
   at least 1 at construction and document that 0 means "keep one", or reject 0 in `PoolCfg` validation.
   Choose the clamp, and assert it, because a panic at startup on a legal-looking configuration value is
   worse than a documented clamp.
7. **All idle connections expired**: `checkout` discards up to 8, then returns `Miss`. Assert exactly 8
   discards and one `Miss` for a queue of 20 expired connections (I-CP4).
8. **Idle timeout of 0**: every connection is immediately expired, so the pool never hits. Legal and
   equivalent to disabling pooling; documented.
9. **`now_ms` wrapping while connections are idle**: `wrapping_sub` is exactly correct for intervals below
   2^32 milliseconds, and the idle timeout is far below that.
10. **`rebuild` with an identical key set**: every slot migrates, no connection is closed. Asserted,
    because this is what happens on a health-only configuration change and closing connections there would
    be a regression.
11. **`rebuild` removing a peer**: that peer's idle connections are closed when the old slots array is
    dropped. Asserted by counting drops with a `C` type that increments a counter in its `Drop`.
12. **`rebuild` shrinking `max_idle_per_peer`**: excess connections are closed during migration.
13. **`rebuild` concurrent with `checkout`**: impossible to observe a mixture, because the index and the
    slot array are one object behind one `ArcSwap`. A checkout sees the whole old generation or the
    whole new one. A connection checked out under the old generation and returned after the swap is
    closed rather than pooled, because its `gen_id` no longer matches. Covered by
    `rebuild_during_checkout_never_leaks_across_peers`.
14. **`PooledConn` dropped without `checkin`**: the connection is closed and `active_conns` is released.
    Asserted with a drop counter.
15. **`PooledConn::take` then drop**: the connection is not closed by the pool (the caller owns it) and
    `active_conns` is still released exactly once.
16. **Two threads checking out the same peer simultaneously**: `ArrayQueue::pop` is MPMC, so each gets a
    different connection or a `Miss`. Never the same connection twice. Covered by
    `loom_concurrent_pop_never_duplicates`.
17. **`adopt` with a slot from a stale `Miss`**: the `gen_id` no longer matches, so `adopt` returns
    `None` and the caller closes the connection itself. A bounds check alone would not be enough here:
    the stale slot number is usually still in range and would name a different peer.
18. **`EndpointId` in the key not present in the registry**: `checkout` cannot acquire a `ConnGuard`, so it
    returns `NotPoolable`. Do not pool a connection whose endpoint has been retired.
19. **`prewarm` with `min_conns == 0`**: returns 0 without calling `dial`, which is the default
    configuration and must cost nothing.
20. **`prewarm` whose `dial` returns `None` on the second call**: one connection is pooled and the
    function returns 1. A dialler that cannot connect must not make `prewarm` loop or retry; it stops
    at the first `None`.
21. **`prewarm` for a key that is not in the index**: returns 0 without calling `dial` and increments
    `unpoolable_count`, exactly like a `checkout` for the same key. Prewarming an unpoolable peer
    would dial connections the pool then immediately closes.
22. **`prewarm` racing a `rebuild`**: `prewarm` loads the generation once, exactly like `checkout`, so
    it either fills the old generation's queue (which is then migrated or dropped by `rebuild`) or the
    new one's. It never pairs a slot number with a slot array from a different generation, which is
    invariant I-CP8.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `cargo test -p irontraffic-upstream pool` passes with the 22 named tests (1 through 21 including
      21a), the `rebuild_during_checkout_never_leaks_across_peers` stress test, and the 3 property tests
      below.
- [ ] `ConnPool::prewarm` and `PoolCfg::min_conns` exist with the signatures given in the Public API
      section and are exercised by test 21a. This issue promises a prewarm entry point in its Context
      and it is the only issue that can define one; a promised entry point with no signature is a
      signature a downstream issue invents differently.
- [ ] `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` contains an "Upstream connection pool" section covering all seven points
      listed in the Design section.
- [ ] `peer_hash` is keyed SipHash-1-3 with a per-process key drawn from the entropy source, asserted by
      test 19. `grep -n "FNV\|fnv" crates/irontraffic-upstream/src/peerkey.rs` prints nothing.
- [ ] The doc comment on `EndpointStats::active_conns` and on `unpoolable_count` state what the Design
      section requires: `active_conns` counts checked-out connections and not open sockets, and
      `unpoolable_count` is an alertable metric because a rising value means a handshake amplifier.
- [ ] `RUSTFLAGS="--cfg loom --cfg crossbeam_loom" cargo test -p irontraffic-upstream --test loom_pool`
      passes, and `grep -n "arc_swap\|ConnPool" crates/irontraffic-upstream/tests/loom_pool.rs` prints
      nothing, because `arc_swap` is not `loom`-instrumented and a `loom` test that loads through it
      verifies nothing.
- [ ] `cargo clippy -p irontraffic-upstream --all-targets -- -D warnings` passes.
- [ ] `grep -rn "Mutex\|RwLock\|\.lock()\|DashMap\|unsafe" crates/irontraffic-upstream/src/pool.rs`
      returns nothing (I-CP6).
- [ ] A counting global allocator test asserts zero allocations across 10000 `checkout` and `checkin`
      pairs (I-CP5).
- [ ] The forged-collision test from edge case 1 exists, is not `#[ignore]`, and asserts that neither key
      receives the other's connection.
- [ ] `PeerKey` derives `PartialEq` and `Eq` structurally over all eight fields, and a test asserts that
      changing each field in turn makes two keys unequal.
- [ ] The module doc comment states that this module is inert until the data-plane issue wires it in, and
      names that issue.

## Tests

Integration tests in `crates/irontraffic-upstream/tests/pool.rs`, using
`struct TestConn { id: u32, closed: Arc<AtomicUsize> }` whose `Drop` increments the counter.

1. `hash_collision_does_not_leak`: two keys forced to the same hash, then pool a connection under key
   A and `checkout(B)`; assert `Miss`, and that `checkout(A)` still returns the pooled connection
   (I-CP1). Force the collision through `#[doc(hidden)] pub fn rebuild_with_hashes(keys, hashes)`,
   compiled unconditionally. It must **not** be `#[cfg(test)]`: this test lives in `tests/pool.rs`,
   which compiles against the crate as an external dependency and cannot see `#[cfg(test)]` items.
2. `sni_difference_is_a_different_peer`: keys identical but for `sni`; a connection pooled under one is not
   returned for the other.
3. `client_cert_difference_is_a_different_peer`.
4. `verify_profile_difference_is_a_different_peer`.
5. `alpn_difference_is_a_different_peer`: this is Pingora's `PreferredHttpVersion` defect.
6. `scheme_difference_is_a_different_peer`.
7. `proxy_difference_is_a_different_peer`.
8. `unknown_peer_is_not_poolable`: a key absent from the index gives `NotPoolable` and increments
   `unpoolable_count`.
9. `checkout_miss_then_adopt_then_checkin_then_hit`: the full lifecycle; assert `idle_len == 1` after
   checkin and `Hit` on the next checkout.
10. `active_conns_balances`: after `adopt`, `active_conns == 1`; after `checkin`, 0; after a subsequent
    `Hit`, 1 again (I-CP2).
11. `queue_full_closes_the_connection`: `max_idle_per_peer = 2`; check in three connections and assert one
    `TestConn` was closed and `idle_len == 2` (I-CP3).
12. `expired_connections_are_discarded_on_checkout`: 20 connections all older than the timeout; assert
    exactly 8 are closed and the result is `Miss` (I-CP4, edge case 7).
13. `evict_sweep_closes_expired`: 4 peers with expired connections; one `evict_idle` closes all of them and
    returns the count.
14. `evict_sweep_keeps_fresh`: fresh connections survive the sweep and `idle_len` is unchanged.
15. `rebuild_migrates_warm_connections`: pool 3 connections, rebuild with the identical key set, assert
    `idle_len == 3` and zero closes (edge case 10).
16. `rebuild_closes_removed_peers`: pool 2 connections for peer A, rebuild without A, assert both are
    closed.
17. `dropped_pooled_conn_closes_and_releases`: `adopt` then drop without `checkin`; assert the connection
    is closed and `active_conns == 0` (edge case 14).
18. `max_idle_zero_is_clamped_not_a_panic`: `PoolCfg { max_idle_per_peer: 0, .. }` constructs successfully
    and behaves as capacity 1 (edge case 6).
19. `peer_hash_is_keyed_per_process`: two `ConnPool` instances constructed in the same process produce
    **different** hashes for the same `PeerKey`, and one instance produces the same hash for the same
    key every time. This is what makes offline bucket grinding impossible, and it fails immediately if
    someone reintroduces a fixed-seed function to make the value reproducible in a test.
20. `colliding_bucket_lookup_stays_correct_and_bounded`: build an index through `rebuild_with_hashes`
    with 64 distinct keys forced onto one hash value. Assert every key still resolves to its own slot
    (the forward walk plus full comparison is correct at any bucket size), and instrument the walk to
    assert it visits at most 64 entries. Then assert the `pool/checkout_hit` benchmark shape is not
    part of this test: this is a correctness test for the pathological case, and the defence against
    the case arising at all is the keyed hash, not a bound on the walk.
21a. `prewarm_fills_to_min_conns`: with `min_conns = 3` and `max_idle_per_peer = 8`, call `prewarm`
    with a dialler that always succeeds; assert it returns 3, that `idle_len == 3`, that
    `active_conns == 0` (a prewarmed connection is idle, not checked out), and that the next
    `checkout` is a `Hit`. Then assert the three `min_conns == 0`, dialler-returns-`None`-second, and
    unknown-key cases from edge cases 19, 20 and 21 in the same test, each asserting the exact return
    value and that `dial` was called the expected number of times, counted by the closure itself.
    Without this test the whole prewarm path is specification with no executable meaning, which is how
    it went unspecified in the first place.
21. `max_idle_per_peer_is_not_the_socket_ceiling`: adopt `max_idle_per_peer` connections, check them
    all in, then check out `max_idle_per_peer` more and adopt that many again. Assert
    `active_conns == max_idle_per_peer` while `idle_len == 0`, and that after checking all of them in
    the peer holds `max_idle_per_peer` idle and `active_conns == 0`. The assertion that matters is the
    documented one: at the intermediate point the process held `2 * max_idle_per_peer` sockets for one
    peer while `active_conns` read half that. This test exists so the invariant's caveat is executable
    rather than a comment.

`loom` tests in `crates/irontraffic-upstream/tests/loom_pool.rs`, run with
`RUSTFLAGS="--cfg loom --cfg crossbeam_loom"`. The second cfg is required: without it
`crossbeam-queue` compiles against `std` atomics that `loom` cannot see, and the model check explores
nothing. **`arc_swap` has no equivalent switch and is never `loom`-instrumented**, so no `loom` test in
this file may call `ConnPool::checkout`, `checkin`, `adopt` or `rebuild`, all of which load
`self.state`. The `loom` tests therefore exercise the one piece that is genuinely concurrent and is
instrumentable, a single `SlotState<C>` plus the `EndpointStats` guard:

- `loom_concurrent_pop_never_duplicates`: one `ArrayQueue` holding one `TestConn`; two threads each
  `pop` and, on success, acquire a `ConnGuard`. Assert exactly one succeeds on every interleaving and
  that the final `active_conns` is 0 once both guards are dropped.
- `loom_queue_balance`: two threads each push then pop then drop a guard against one `SlotState` and
  one `EndpointStats`; assert `active_conns` returns to 0 and the queue length returns to its starting
  value on every interleaving (I-CP2).

The generation-swap property is not `loom`-checkable for the reason above, so it is asserted by an
ordinary threaded stress test in `tests/pool.rs` instead, which is a real test rather than a vacuous
one:

- `rebuild_during_checkout_never_leaks_across_peers`: two peers whose slot numbers **swap** between two
  generations (arrange this with `rebuild_with_hashes` so the sort order flips). One thread calls
  `rebuild` alternating between the two key orders 10000 times; four threads each perform
  `checkout`, `adopt` on a `Miss`, and `checkin`, 10000 times, with each thread using one fixed peer
  key and a `TestConn` tagged with that peer. Assert that no thread is ever handed a `TestConn` tagged
  with the other peer, that no index is out of range, and that `active_conns` for both endpoints is 0
  once every thread has joined and every `PooledConn` is dropped (I-CP8).

Property tests (`proptest`):

- `pool_key_equality_is_field_wise`: for arbitrary `PeerKey` pairs, `a == b` if and only if every field is
  equal. Generated over all eight fields including `None` and `Some("")` for the optional strings.
- `pool_never_exceeds_capacity`: for arbitrary sequences of up to 200 `adopt` and `checkin` operations
  across up to 8 peers, `idle_len` never exceeds `max_idle_per_peer` and the total closed plus total idle
  equals the total adopted (I-CP3).
- `pool_checkout_is_bounded`: for arbitrary queues of expired and fresh connections, `checkout` performs at
  most `CHECKOUT_EXPIRY_BUDGET` discards (I-CP4).

## Benchmarks

On the request path.

`crates/irontraffic-upstream/benches/pool.rs`, criterion:

- `pool/checkout_hit/{P}` for `P` in `{1, 100, 10000}` peers. Gate: **under 60 ns at `P = 10000`**,
  which is 14 levels of binary search over an 80 KB `u64` array (mostly L2, a few LLC), plus one full
  key comparison, plus one queue pop. Meeting it requires the parallel-array `PeerIndex`: a search
  over an array of 104-byte structs touches roughly 1 MB and costs three times as much.
- `pool/checkout_miss/{P}`: same shape without a pop. Gate: **under 40 ns at `P = 10000`**.
- `pool/checkin`: Gate: **under 30 ns**.
- `pool/key_hash`: one `peer_hash` over a key with a 32-byte SNI. Gate: **under 30 ns**.
- `pool/full_key_compare`: one `PeerKey` equality comparison. Reported, ungated; the point of the
  measurement is that it is small enough that the correction is free, and the documentation quotes it.
- `pool/rebuild/{P}` for `P` in `{100, 10000}`. Reported, ungated; configuration-plane only.

## Do NOT

- Do NOT key the pool by a hash, a `u64`, or anything other than the full `PeerKey`, and do NOT skip the
  full comparison on a hit. That is the defect this issue corrects.
- Do NOT use `DashMap`, `Mutex`, `RwLock`, `parking_lot`, or any lock. `ArrayQueue` plus `arc_swap`.
- Do NOT put the peer index and the slot array behind two separate `ArcSwap`s. One object, one swap:
  a reader that pairs a new slot number with an old slot array gets another peer's connection, and no
  bounds check can detect it.
- Do NOT make the pool per worker or per core.
- Do NOT bias endpoint selection toward endpoints with warm connections. That is a load-balancing decision
  and it belongs to the picker, which deliberately does not consider warmth.
- Do NOT grow the index from request-derived keys. An unknown key is `NotPoolable`.
- Do NOT use an unkeyed hash for the pool index. The bytes include tenant-supplied SNI and proxy
  strings, and `lookup` walks forward over every colliding entry, so an unkeyed function lets a tenant
  grind one bucket and turn every checkout in the process into a linear scan. The hash is
  process-local, so keying it costs nothing.
- Do NOT let a request-derived value reach a `PeerKey` without being resolved against the configured
  key set. A varying SNI forces a full TLS handshake per request, which is roughly a thousandfold CPU
  amplification against this proxy and the upstream both.
- Do NOT describe `active_conns` as the number of open connections. It counts checked-out ones; idle
  pooled connections hold sockets and no guard.
- Do NOT allocate in `checkout` or `checkin`.
- Do NOT let a checkout walk an unbounded number of expired connections.
- Do NOT drop all connections on a peer-set rebuild. Migrate the ones whose keys survive.
- Do NOT add a public method that decrements `active_conns`. `ConnGuard::drop` is the only path.
- Do NOT dial, connect, handshake, or negotiate a protocol in this module. It stores opaque values of type
  `C`.
- Do NOT count `active_conns` as the load-balancing signal. In-flight **requests** are the signal;
  `active_conns` is for pool limits only, because one HTTP/2 connection carries many concurrent streams.

