## Fresh world pressure

Public AI discussion is increasingly framed around promised future benefits versus observable present transitions: employment, ownership/distribution, surveillance, military use, concentration of authority, self-regulation and deferred claims of abundance/health benefits.

Use that as quarry, not validation.

A useful pressure form is:

```
PROMISED FUTURE STATE
!=
OBSERVED TRANSITION PATH

GOOD DESTINATION CLAIM
does not erase
WHO BEARS THE TRANSITION / WHAT HARDENS NOW / WHAT REMAINS REACHABLE
```

Do not turn this into an AI-good/AI-bad argument.

## Questions to attack

1. Can harm/value be derived from the substrate plus explicit bindings, or must some normative quantity remain primitive?
2. Does reachability/hardening remain useful in mundane cases, or only dramatic irreversible ones?
3. How does the grammar behave when several affected parties want incompatible futures?
4. What survives when there is no clear chooser: markets, crowds, ecosystems, institutions, stochastic systems?
5. Who has standing to bind baseline/value/horizon when no authority is uncontested?
